e is a compiled language with algebraic composition rules. \n\n1. [The size of the claim] Every account of life now in use is chemical. Life is a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution, which describes what living things do across generations and says nothing about what any one of them is at any instant, and everyone in the field knows it is a placeholder. Schr\\"odinger\'s aperiodic crystal and the autopoietic account are the two serious attempts at something better, and neither became operational, because neither could be computed. You cannot hand a molecule to the autopoietic definition and get an answer. Life is a compiled language with algebraic compositi\n\n2. [The lexer] Twelve of the twenty amino acids carry a structural role and eight do not. The twelve stand in one-to-one correspondence with twelve operations a structure performs on itself, and the correspondence is fixed, not fitted. Twenty-three of the sixty-one sense codons are therefore significant and the rest are passed over, which is what a lexer does with whitespace. A reading frame yields a word in these twelve by emitting the mark of each promoted codon and nothing for the rest.\n\n3. [The grammar] Two of the twelve divide and rejoin; the remaining ten are boundaries, self-reference, and the seven that transform what they act on. The division between marks that do work and marks that do not is what the type rule turns on, and it is the plainest division available: a boundary opens or closes a reading and leaves the state alone, self-reference returns what it was given, and the division and the rejoining move structure without transforming it. Words are read as loops, the last mark followed by the first. This is forced. A word read from an arbitrary cut makes a rejoining whose division li\n\n4. [The type system] A word divides and rejoins, or fails to, and the failures are not one failure. It closes when every division is answered and at least one answered region contains work. It hangs open when a division is never answered. It is ill-typed when a rejoining answers to no division. It is null when divisions and rejoinings answer each other but nothing between them does work. Four judgements, because two will not do. The values are those of the Belnap lattice , which records not only truth and falsity but the two ways a determination fails, by holding too much and by holding too little. Null and ill-ty\n\n5. [The type rule] [Pairing] Write b(i) for the number of divisions minus the number of rejoinings among the first i marks of a word w of length n , so b(0) = 0 and b(n) = d , the difference of the two counts. Let k be an index at which b attains its minimum. Read w as a loop beginning at k ; on each division push its position onto a stack, and on each rejoining pop, forming a pair whose region runs forward around the loop. A rejoining that finds the stack empty is unmatched, and positions left on the stack are unmatched divisions. [Surplus] For any word, the unmatched divisions number (0, d) and the unmatched r\n\n6. [The judgement in codons] The division is asparagine and the rejoining is glutamine, whose codon families are AAY and CAR . They differ in the first base alone, adenine dividing and cytosine rejoining, with adenine fixed at the second position. One pass over a frame decides it, with no alignment, no homologs, no training set, no energy function and no coordinates.\n\n7. […] A compiler fixes its judgement at a particular moment, and everything after that moment is execution. Here the judgement is computed from the promoted residues and from nothing else, so it is fixed when the ribosome finishes. Folding does not determine it and cannot revise it. The unread redundancy sits where it does in every compiled language. The third base carries the bulk of the code\'s degeneracy and no structural weight: within the \\ A,C\\ A\\ U,C,A,G\\ block it only chooses between the two families sharing the first two positions, so that AAY 